# hey-eve settings
#
# Copy this to ~/.config/eve/env and edit it:
#
#     mkdir -p ~/.config/eve && chmod 700 ~/.config/eve
#     cp env.example ~/.config/eve/env && chmod 600 ~/.config/eve/env
#
# deploy/install.sh does that for you and fills in the answers it asks for.
#
# This file is data, not shell code: one KEY=value per line, with no export or
# substitution. It is never sourced, so values are not interpreted as shell
# syntax.
#
# It is read in two ways, and the difference is the security design.
#
#   * The two credentials below are read on demand by config.secret() and are
#     not put into the environment, so they are not inherited by aplay,
#     arecord, or whisper.cpp.
#   * Documented non-secret settings are applied to the process environment
#     when the package is imported.
#
# For a direct run, a value already present in the process environment wins
# over this file. A credential supplied that way can be inherited by child
# processes, so the owner-only file is the supported service configuration.
# install.sh deliberately ignores inherited assistant and uv project-selection
# settings other than the EVE_CONFIG_DIR locator, then pins uv to this checkout
# and its .venv. Put persistent installer/service values in the selected file.

# --- credentials ----------------------------------------------------------

# Required for language-model turns.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# Optional. Barkeep is the control plane for the BUSY Bar; with a token here
# the assistant is offered a tool that reports what the bar is showing.
# Leave it blank and that tool is not offered at all — every call would
# otherwise come back as an auth error the model would read aloud.
# On the host that runs Barkeep: grep ^BARKEEP_TOKEN ~/busybar-lab/.env
BARKEEP_TOKEN=

# Where Barkeep listens. A fresh Barkeep install binds loopback HTTP. A
# non-loopback URL must use HTTPS and pass normal certificate verification;
# install a private or self-signed certificate in the host trust store rather
# than disabling verification. Loopback may use Barkeep's generated
# self-signed HTTPS certificate.
BARKEEP_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8080

# --- how she presents -----------------------------------------------------

# `female` or `male`; selects the default Kokoro voice when VOICE_TTS_VOICE is
# unset.
VOICE_PRESENT=female

# Any voice in the Kokoro bank; `uv run scripts/audition.py` plays them.
# Unset means the voice that matches VOICE_PRESENT.
# VOICE_TTS_VOICE=bf_emma

# Kokoro speech speed, from 0.5 to 2.0. Faster speech also shortens synthesis
# time because it produces less audio.
# VOICE_TTS_SPEED=1.0

# Model filename inside KOKORO_DIR. The supported installer and doctor command
# verify kokoro-v1.0.onnx; another filename is an unsupported development
# override and will fail the production runtime check.
# KOKORO_MODEL=kokoro-v1.0.onnx

# ONNX threads for synthesis. 0 leaves thread selection to ONNX Runtime.
# VOICE_TTS_THREADS=0

# Reply persona: `glados` or `plain`.
VOICE_PERSONA=glados

# Optional name the assistant may use when addressing or referring to you.
# VOICE_USER=

# --- the model ------------------------------------------------------------

# Haiku is the low-latency default. Set `claude-sonnet-5` for a more capable
# model with higher latency and API cost.
VOICE_MODEL=claude-haiku-4-5

# Web search is an Anthropic server-side tool. An organization administrator
# can disable or restrict it in the Anthropic Console, and Anthropic bills
# searches separately.

# --- audio ----------------------------------------------------------------

# Which microphone. Leave it blank and she finds one: the first capture card
# `arecord -l` reports, wrapped in plughw: so ALSA converts the rate rather
# than refusing a device that cannot do 16kHz natively. She says which one she
# picked at startup.
#
# Name one here to override that. `arecord -l` lists the candidates.
#
# Note that `default` is usually the wrong answer on this kind of box: with a
# Bluetooth speaker pinned as ALSA's default, the *capture* side of `default`
# resolves to bluealsa and will not open at all.
VOICE_MIC=

# Silence, in seconds, before she decides you have finished talking.
VOICE_HANG_S=2.0

# Seconds of unbroken speech treated as a stuck source. Brief gaps reset the
# counter, so this is not a normal utterance-length limit. 0 disables it.
VOICE_RUNAWAY_S=120

# Wake-word-free window after a reply, measured to the moment you START
# talking — thinking time, not talking time.
VOICE_FOLLOW_UP_S=60

# Hard limits for one capture and one transcription. These also bound sources
# such as radio or television audio that contain frequent brief pauses.
# VOICE_MAX_CAPTURE_S=300
# VOICE_TRANSCRIBE_TIMEOUT_S=180

# SDK timeout applied to each model API request attempt.
# VOICE_API_TIMEOUT_S=30

# Output gain from 0.0 to 1.0, applied after speech normalization and to the
# acknowledgement tone. The default is 1.0.
# VOICE_VOLUME=1.0

# Bluetooth amplifiers sleep. After this much quiet, pad the front of the
# next reply with silence so the first word is not swallowed by the wake-up.
VOICE_SPEAKER_IDLE_S=45
VOICE_SPEAKER_WAKE_S=1.5

# Optional Bluetooth speaker address. The installer uses it to enable a timer
# that reconnects the device after boot or link loss. `bluetoothctl devices
# Paired` lists candidates. Leave blank for wired/default ALSA playback.
VOICE_SPEAKER_MAC=

# --- being heard, and being stopped ---------------------------------------

# A short acknowledgement tone after a transcript has matched the wake phrase.
# Disable it or adjust its frequency, duration, and amplitude below.
# VOICE_EARCON=0        turns it off
# VOICE_EARCON_HZ=660
# VOICE_EARCON_S=0.12
# VOICE_EARCON_LEVEL=0.25

# Eve reads an ALSA capture switch when the microphone exposes one. Otherwise
# it infers missing signal from this RMS threshold. Measure the device's muted
# and quiet-room levels before changing the default.
# VOICE_SILENT_RMS=1.0
# VOICE_MUTE_AFTER_S=3.0      how long before the panel says MUTED

# And how long before a muted microphone, or sustained silence when no capture
# switch is available, moves the face into its visible sleep animation. The
# renderer keeps drawing so sleep remains distinguishable from a crashed or
# disconnected display. Pending reminders are held until signal returns.
# VOICE_SLEEP_AFTER_S=600

# Barge-in stops playback when new speech is detected. It is disabled until
# calibrated because speaker echo depends on the room, amplifier, and
# microphone. Run with VOICE_BARGE_CALIBRATE=1 for several replies in an empty
# room, set VOICE_BARGE_RATIO above the largest logged ratio, then enable
# VOICE_BARGE_IN. An uncalibrated threshold can stop replies accidentally.
# VOICE_BARGE_IN=1
# VOICE_BARGE_CALIBRATE=1
# VOICE_BARGE_RATIO=2.5       how much louder than herself counts as you
# VOICE_BARGE_FLOOR=300       and an absolute floor, so the gaps between her
#                             own words cannot trigger on room tone
# VOICE_BARGE_CHUNKS=5        consecutive 32ms chunks; a door bang is brief

# --- the touchscreen ------------------------------------------------------

# Set to 0 to disable touchscreen discovery and input.
# VOICE_TOUCH=0         leaves the panel alone entirely

# Touch orientation depends on the panel assembly. Run the calibration helper:
#
#     uv run python scripts/touch-probe.py
#
# Touch three corners when asked and paste the three printed settings here.
# Axis ranges are read from the input device.
# VOICE_TOUCH_SWAP=0
# VOICE_TOUCH_FLIP_X=0
# VOICE_TOUCH_FLIP_Y=0

# --- paths ----------------------------------------------------------------

# Paths beginning with ~ are expanded. Relative model and Whisper paths are
# resolved from the repository checkout by the installer, service, doctor,
# and deploy script.
#
# A managed systemd install validates the checkout, EVE_CONFIG_DIR, uv
# executable, and uv cache paths, then embeds them in the generated unit. It
# also pins uv's project environment to the checkout's .venv. Those paths may
# use only ASCII letters, digits, /, ., _, +, and -; the installer rejects
# whitespace, %, quotes, and backslashes before changing the environment.

# Shared model root. Silero lives under models/ and Kokoro under kokoro/.
# EVE_MODELS_DIR=~/.local/share/eve

# Explicit Kokoro directory override. When unset, this is
# EVE_MODELS_DIR/kokoro.
# KOKORO_DIR=~/.local/share/eve/kokoro

# Managed whisper.cpp source/build/model checkout.
# WHISPER_DIR=~/whisper.cpp

# Configuration directory. This must be supplied to install.sh or in the
# process environment because a line inside the file cannot relocate the file
# that must be found before it is read. The generated systemd unit records the
# resolved value.
# EVE_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/eve

# Pre-release installations may still use HEY_CLAUDE_MODELS,
# HEY_CLAUDE_CONFIG, or ~/.local/share/hey-claude. The compatibility aliases
# remain supported and the installer can move legacy model data.

# --- debugging ------------------------------------------------------------

# Permit transcript and reply content in process logs. For the managed service,
# this writes content to journald until its retention policy removes it. Eve
# also prints content during an interactive terminal run; terminal scrollback
# or session logging may retain that output.
# VOICE_DEBUG=1
